SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: the approximation of a walked surface/restriction blend solves
the sections between the walk's points on the walked ball, not on the
ball on the restriction's other side (mitcad#2161).

Defect. A plate with a round step 0.2 high, the step's foot rounded with
0.4 (higher than the step, the ball rolling on the top rim, patch 0011).
Expected: the rolling ball's blend all round. Observed: with the step's
radius 100 or more (radius 150 for a rounding of 0.6, 200 for 1) the
fillet failed, "PerformSurf : Failed approximation!": StoreData found no
sample of the blend with a normal longer than the approximation's
reached tolerance, which was 0.326 mm (4e-6 at radius 50).

Cause. Each section of a surface/restriction blend has a second
solution, the ball on the restriction's other side, its contact on the
surface 2c from the walked one (c the contact's distance from the
restriction across the surface, 0.346 here). The walk keeps to its
branch with steps of 9.6 degrees: its deflection allowance is a
thousandth of the guide's length, the same angles at any radius.
BRepBlend_AppFuncRoot::SearchPoint starts each section between two walked
points from their linear interpolation; on the surface that start lies on
the chord, inside the contact circle by its sagitta, (R + c) theta^2 / 8
= 0.35 at radius 100, past the middle between the two balls. The solver
went over to the other ball (its plate contact at 99.654 instead of
100.346), those sections were inserted into the line (more than three
iterations) and drew more starts over, and the approximation joined the
two branches.

- BRepBlend_HermiteContact: the contact on the surface at the section's
  parameter from the walked points about it that carry the contact's
  tangent (dU/dt, dV/dt along the guide, as the walk stores them), a
  cubic Hermite interpolation: its error is of the fourth order in the
  step, the chord's of the second. Points the approximation inserted
  carry no tangent and are passed over.
- SearchPoint, for a surface/restriction blend (three variables), where
  the solution from the linear start lies farther from that contact than
  the start did (and more than ten times the variables' tolerance from
  it), solves the section again from that contact, the restriction's
  parameter as before, and takes the second solution where it lies
  nearer to the contact; otherwise the first, solved again so that the
  function's state is its own. The insertion of the point into the line
  follows the solution taken.
Elsewhere the linear start's solution lies on the walked contact, nearer
to the Hermite contact than the chord, and is taken as before. Blends
between two surfaces and between two restrictions are not changed.

Upstream: new.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 1870.

